c. enhancement of efforts to protect women from various acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination, including prevention and mitigation efforts;
d. development of gender-responsive policies, monitoring of gender-based violence, increase in budget allocation for Women's Empowerment, and for improving women's welfare in each sector and field;
e. development and improvement of regulations and policies of PKHP in various fields of development;
f. implementation of communication, information, and education (KIE) for improving the quality of life of women.
g. preparation of recording and reporting systems, and systems for handling and resolving cases of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
h. development of integrated service centers based in hospitals, and community-based centers in Regions, as facilities for protection of women victims of violence, including women victims of domestic violence.

Article 22
(1) Regional Governments have the duty to implement efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children, comprising:
a. managing data and information regarding the protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values toward women and children; and
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Regional Governments in carrying out duties as referred to in paragraph (1), have the authority to provide and organize services for victims of violence, among others:
a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims of acts of violence by involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for women and children; and
c. conducting community empowerment for the prevention of acts of violence against women and children.

Article 12
The Regent conducts guidance on the implementation of PUG which includes:
a. establishment of technical guidelines for the implementation of PUG at the Regency, Sub-district, and Village scale;
b. strengthening of institutional capacity through training, consultation, advocacy, and coordination;
c. monitoring and evaluation of PUG implementation in the Region;
d. capacity building of PUG Working Group and focal point; and
e. performance achievement strategy.

Article 14
Regional Government implements policies, programs, and activities of PKHP in various development sectors, through:
a. coordination of implementation of policies, programs, and activities of PKHP among SKPD;
b. cooperation with other Regional Governments in the implementation of policies, programs, and activities of PKHP;
c. strengthening of institutional capacity of PUG for the implementation of PKHP; d. facilitation of PKHP services;
e. provision of PKHP services;
f. implementation of PKHP affirmative action; and
g. preparation of PKHP data collection system.

Article 8
(1) The PUG Working Group as referred to in Article 7, has the following duties:
a. promoting and facilitating PUG to each SKPD;
b. conducting socialization and advocacy of PUG to District Heads and Village Heads;
c. preparing work programs every year;
d. encouraging the realization of Gender Responsive Planning and Budgeting;
e. preparing the work plan of the PUG Working Group every year;
f. being responsible to the Regent;
g. formulating policy recommendations to the Regent;
h. preparing the Regional Gender Profile;
i. monitoring the implementation of PUG in each agency;
j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of the regional budget;
k. preparing the Regional Action Plan (RAD) for PUG in the Region; and
l. encouraging the implementation of selection and determination of Focal Points in each SKPD.
(2) The Technical Team as referred to in paragraph (1) letter j shall consist of officials who understand Gender Responsive Budget analysis.
(3) The Regional Action Plan (RAD) for PUG as referred to in paragraph (1) letter k shall contain:
a. PUG in laws and regulations in the Region;
b. PUG in the development cycle in the Region;
c. institutional strengthening of PUG in the Region; and
d. strengthening community participation in the Region.

